# Silence "Failed to start Attach File /dev/block/sr0 to Loopback Block Device."
# on the live medium.
#
# systemd's /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/99-systemd.rules pulls in systemd-loop@.service
# for an optical drive whose GPT the kernel could not parse, so that
# systemd-gpt-auto-generator can still see discoverable partitions:
#
#   SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_CDROM}=="1", \
#     ENV{ID_PART_GPT_AUTO_ROOT_DISK_NEEDS_LOOP}=="1", \
#     ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="systemd-loop@.service"
#
# An El Torito ISO booted from a 2048-byte-sector CD-ROM always trips that
# check, and `systemd-dissect --attach` then fails on the read-only device, so
# the job lands on the console as a red [FAILED] line during boot.
#
# archiso never uses gpt-auto root: its own initramfs hook finds the medium by
# the archisosearchuuid= / archisodevice= boot parameters and mounts the
# squashfs from there. The loop device buys nothing here, so clear the flag
# before 99-systemd.rules reads it. The blkid builtin that sets the property
# runs from 60-persistent-storage.rules, so 90- lands between the two.
ACTION!="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_CDROM}=="1", \
  ENV{ID_PART_GPT_AUTO_ROOT_DISK_NEEDS_LOOP}="0"
